/*
 * /terms — the Terms of Service.
 *
 * WHAT THIS DOCUMENT IS DRAFTED TO. The operator's instruction, verbatim: "we're promising
 * nothing and guaranteeing nothing, it should be written very much in our favor." Every
 * clause below is written to that brief. Where a clause is SOFTER than the brief, it is
 * because the aggressive version is void on its face and an unenforceable term protects
 * nobody. Those places are marked in this block so nobody "fixes" them back:
 *
 *   1. LIABILITY IS STATED AS ZERO, WITH US$100 AS A SEVERABILITY FALLBACK ONLY.
 *      ★ SUPERSEDED (operator, 2026-07-27), kept so nobody reinstates it: this block used to
 *      read "THE LIABILITY CAP IS US$100, NOT ZERO", on the reasoning that a zero cap reads
 *      as a total exclusion of remedy and US courts strike those more readily than a nominal
 *      floor. The operator was told that and reaffirmed the brief twice: no guarantee, no
 *      liability, total use at your own risk. THAT LINE IS DEAD.
 *      The clause now STATES zero and falls back to the greater of US$100 / fees paid only
 *      where a jurisdiction forbids total exclusion. That is strictly more favourable than a
 *      flat $100 cap and loses nothing: where total exclusion is allowed it is zero, and
 *      where it is not, severability lands on the same nominal floor the old text conceded
 *      up front. The enforceability concern is answered by the fallback, not ignored.
 *
 *   2. THE UNWAIVABLE CARVE-OUTS ARE STATED, NOT OMITTED. Fraud, gross negligence, willful
 *      misconduct and death or personal injury caused by negligence cannot be disclaimed in
 *      most US states, and a disclaimer that reaches them can take the enforceable part of
 *      the clause down with it. Naming them as exceptions is what keeps the rest standing.
 *
 *   3. DISPUTES GO TO COURT, NOT TO ARBITRATION, and this was a judgment call the operator
 *      should review. Arbitration is the usual maximally-protective choice, but each filing
 *      costs a company four figures in administrative and arbitrator fees REGARDLESS OF
 *      MERIT, and there is no revenue here to absorb a mass-filing campaign against a free
 *      product with no payment relationship to deter one. The class-action waiver — which is
 *      the thing arbitration is normally used to secure — is written into the forum clause
 *      instead, alongside a jury waiver and an informal-resolution step. Honest limitation:
 *      a class waiver outside an arbitration agreement is enforced less uniformly than one
 *      inside it. Switching to arbitration is a live option; it is a cost decision.
 *
 *   4. NO SECTION IS REFERRED TO BY NUMBER. LegalDoc numbers sections positionally, so
 *      inserting a clause renumbers everything. Cross-references are by name and link.
 *
 *   5. TEXAS LAW AND TRAVIS COUNTY VENUE ARE A DECISION, NOT AN ARTEFACT — and they are
 *      deliberately NOT the state of formation. Yulan Ventures, LLC is formed in
 *      WASHINGTON and its place of business is in AUSTIN, TEXAS (operator-confirmed
 *      2026-07-28). An earlier draft picked Texas by INFERRING Texas formation from the
 *      Austin address; that inference was wrong and the formation clause has been
 *      corrected. The venue choice survives the correction on its own merits, so it is
 *      restated here rather than left looking like a leftover:
 *        - Venue should be where we would actually appear. That is Austin. Selecting
 *          Washington would mean litigating in a state where the company has a filing
 *          and no people.
 *        - Texas enforces forum-selection and limitation-of-liability clauses readily,
 *          which is what the rest of this document depends on.
 *      NO CONFLICT WITH THE FORMATION STATE. Washington law governs the LLC's INTERNAL
 *      affairs — member rights, manager duties — under the internal-affairs doctrine,
 *      whatever this document says. These Terms govern something else entirely: the
 *      company's relationship with a USER. A contract may select its own governing law,
 *      and the two never meet.
 *      If the office moves, revisit this; if the formation state changes, do not.
 *
 * WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT: payment, refund, subscription, renewal, price-change and
 * chargeback terms. There is no billing, no paid tier and no payment processor anywhere in
 * this product, and drafting terms for machinery that does not exist invites a reader to
 * believe it does.
 *
 * FACTS IN THIS DOCUMENT ARE READ OUT OF THE CODE, NOT ASSUMED:
 *   3 live workspaces per verified identity  -> FREE_TIER_WORKSPACE_LIMIT,
 *                                               supabase/functions/command/index.ts:330
 *   120 signals / credential / hour          -> SIGNAL_CREDENTIAL_LIMIT, :297
 *   1000 signals / workspace / hour          -> SIGNAL_WORKSPACE_LIMIT, :298
 *   hourly windows                           -> incrementSignalBucket, :2342
 *   signal body 1-2000 chars, about <= 500,
 *     lifetime <= 30 days                    -> supabase/migrations/20260724000003_signals.sql
 *   a signal never claims or blocks anything -> AGENTS.md, and the reducer: posting a signal
 *                                               writes no lease and closes no task
 *
 * NOTES GO IN THIS BLOCK, NEVER IN THE MARKUP — Astro ships <!-- comments --> verbatim.
 */

